  "title": "# Build: terminal-drag-chain-tree (Current profile) ## Brett said (verbatim, Nov 3 2026, voice) \"Th…",
  "asked": "# Build: terminal-drag-chain-tree (Current profile) ## Brett said (verbatim, Nov 3 2026, voice) \"There's nowhere to click to drag the terminal menu, the terminal window around. There's nowhere to click. It's all tabbed.\" \"In the context chain in terminal for the MD files on the headless sessions, it needs to show them like a tree. So every file that is chained, it needs to open indented.\" Assigned agent: whoever is free (router if on main; else **Landin' Lenny**; record who). Register in the `current` profile (id `terminal-drag-chain-tree`) + banner entry. window-resize-fix (#278) just landed: build on it. ## 1. Drag the Terminal window The frameless Terminal window uses its tab strip as the title bar, and the strip is full of tabs, so there's no empty spot to grab. Fix in the framework window host (every frameless window, every app): an always-present drag area that tabs can never cover - a slim grab handle/grip at the left of the tab strip (and/or a small spacer before the window buttons), plus: long-press (phone) or press-and-hold (desktop) on any tab and move = drag the window (a quick tap still switches tabs; tab reorder needs a horizontal drag inside the strip), and double-tap the handle = maximize/restore. Touch targets >= 44 px on phone. Works with the stacked-tabs setting coming in terminal-tabs-left (left stack: handle at the top of the stack). ## 2. Context chain as a tree In the headless tab's split pane B (headless-tabs s7: the CLAUDE.md / rules chain revealed one rule at a time), show the chain as a TREE: each MD file that loads another file appears as a child, indented under the file that pulled it in (CLAUDE.md -> _claude/rules/RULES.md -> shared rules -> project rules -> instructions/... -> memory/...), with expand/collapse per node, file icons, the reason it's loaded (import / reference / session-start rule), and a count of rules per file. Selecting a node shows that file's content with the slow reveal; the reveal walks the tree depth-first. Build the tree from the real load order (parse @imports / 'read X' references / the session-start list), not a hard-coded list. Also show it this way in the console-terminal headless chain read (`console-terminal/headless/{name}/chain` returns a nested tree). ## Done Tests (drag via handle + long-press on tab vs tap/reorder, tree building from nested imports), phone + desktop screenshots, UI Review item, report `_claude/builds/reports/terminal-drag-chain-tree.md`, THREADS, PR, steps through the store. Commits clean and professional. ## Addendum 1 (Brett, Nov 3 2026, voice) Brett said (verbatim): \"And I need the context menu on the terminal to open its settings.\" - Right-click / long-press anywhere on the Terminal window (tab strip, drag handle, terminal body margin, empty area) shows a context menu with 'Terminal settings' at the top, opening them where the Terminal's 'Opens in' setting says (dialog / main; in-plugin pane once plugin-settings-placement lands). Keep the terminal's own text right-click (copy/paste) working: inside the terminal text area, add 'Terminal settings' to that menu too. Also fix the 'Opens in' description wording (it now controls where settings open, not just 'Open a tab'). ## Addendum 2 (Brett, Nov 3 2026, voice) Brett said (verbatim): \"I need to be able to change the size of the text in the main window here on the left side. It's too big. I need to be able to change the size of the text.\" - Text size control for every Terminal pane, starting with the main/left pane (the terminal text and the split's left pane, and the rules/chain pane): a 'Text size' setting per pane + a global Terminal default (font size, line height, font family), quick A-/A+ buttons in the Terminal toolbar, pinch-to-zoom on phone and Ctrl/Cmd +/- / Ctrl+wheel on desktop, reset to default. Remembered per device and per tab/profile. Current default is too big on the phone: lower the phone default (from the typography/density tokens, not hard-coded). Watch live: Brett can watch …",
